module gunbc.v2_rustc_debt

import std.types { String, Int, List, Bool }

// THE MONOTONE IDENTITY LEDGER FOR RUSTC DIAGNOSTICS OVER THE EMITTED v2 CRATE.
//
// WHY A LEDGER AND NOT A NUMBER. A checked-in integer -- "do not exceed 193" -- admits three
// changes it should refuse and DESIGN's own oracle rule already forbids it: a measurement copied
// from the current tree is not an oracle, and completeness is an identity join rather than a
// count equality. Concretely, a count lets one error disappear while another appears, lets a
// repaired error's allowance be spent on a new one, and lets debt return after an improvement.
// Every one of those is a real regression that a total cannot see.
//
// THE FOUR CONDITIONS DESIGN SETS FOR A MONOTONE DEBT CONTRACT ARE MET HERE AND ARE WORTH
// NAMING INDIVIDUALLY, because the same shape authored without them is the quieter snapshot the
// rule rejects: the subject universe is INDEPENDENTLY DISCOVERED (rustc emits it from the
// emitted crate; nobody authors the population), membership is checked AT IDENTITY GRAIN
// (file, code) rather than by count, the ROADMAP direction is the v2 self-host, and every
// removal carries a typed disposition below.
//
// THE KEY IS NOT THE MESSAGE, and that is a decision rather than an economy. A rustc message
// carries the offending spelling, so keying on it would make an identical defect at an
// identical site a NEW identity every time a name near it changed -- a ledger that churns on
// renames stops being read. It is also not the primary span's absolute path, which is
// machine-specific: two runners produce two identities for one defect. The key is the emitted
// crate-relative source path and the rustc code.

data v2_rustc_debt_key_law: String = "A RustcDebtKey is (normalized emitted-crate-relative primary-source path, rustc diagnostic code). The path is relative to the emitted crate root, never absolute, because an absolute path is a property of the machine that compiled and would mint a fresh identity per runner. The code is rustc's own (E0599, E0308) and is upstream's name for the class, not one coined here -- DESIGN section 3, cite the source and keep its real names. A diagnostic carrying NO code, or no span that normalizes to a crate-relative path, is NOT given a synthesized key: it refuses, because a fabricated identity would silently join to whatever row it happened to collide with."

data v2_rustc_debt_admission_law: String = "Admission is exactly: observed == admitted_here, AND admitted_here is a multiplicity-wise subset of admitted_at_baseline. The first relation refuses a new error with no ledger edit and refuses a stale allowance left behind by a repair; the second refuses a ledger increase, including one that matches a real regression, because the baseline does not contain it. Together they make every reduction PERMANENT, which is the property a ceiling does not have. Neither total participates: occurrences and buckets are printed as receipts and are read by humans, never by the verdict."

data v2_rustc_debt_bucket_grain_law: String = "THIS IS A PER-FILE-PER-CODE MULTIPLICITY RATCHET, NOT AN INDIVIDUAL ERROR IDENTITY, and the distinction is stated here because an earlier revision of this module called the key an identity throughout and its test suite carried an arm named for the general swap case while exercising only the special one. WHAT IT CATCHES: a swap across files, a swap across codes, and multiplicity growth within one bucket. WHAT IT DOES NOT CATCH: equal-multiplicity substitution INSIDE one bucket -- fix one E0308 in a file, introduce a different E0308 in the same file, and the key and the multiplicity are both unchanged, so the contract admits it. That blind spot is executed rather than described, by test.claim.v2_rustc_debt_ratchet_test the_same_bucket_substitution_blind_spot_is_admitted, which asserts ADMISSION and therefore goes red the day the key descends -- the boundary is a measured fact with a regression control, not a caveat in prose. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: an emitter sidecar mapping emitted Rust byte ranges back to a stable Daglang origin (module identity, declaration identity, occurrence identity), keyed as (emission_unit, cargo_target, dag_source_origin, rustc_code). That survives line movement and emitted-file re-layout, which a Rust line/column coordinate does not; diagnostic text is not a candidate because a spelling change is not an identity change. A SECOND UNCARRIED DIMENSION, same shape and different cause: the observation runs --all-targets, so one source may compile under several cargo targets and a TARGET-GRAPH change can move multiplicity with no source defect changing. The key must carry the cargo target name and kind, or the observer must deduplicate across targets deliberately rather than by accident."

data v2_rustc_debt_baseline_law: String = "The baseline ledger is read at the repository's authoritative diff baseline via gunbc.diff_baseline, including its direct-versus-merge-base distinction -- never a hardcoded origin/main, which is the policy-as-literal fork that module exists to have dissolved. Reading ONE FILE from the baseline tree is what this needs; recompiling the baseline tree is not, and the difference is why the contract is stated over the LEDGER rather than over the observation."

// (normalized emitted-crate-relative path, rustc code). Both components are required; there is
// no arm for a diagnostic that supplied neither, because the refusal happens at observation.
//
// THE NAME SAYS KEY AND NOT IDENTITY DELIBERATELY -- see `v2_rustc_debt_bucket_grain_law`. Two
// distinct errors of one code in one file share this key and are indistinguishable to every
// relation below it.
type RustcDebtKey {
  source_path: String,
  code: String
}

// MULTIPLICITY IS AN INT AND NOT A BOOL, because two occurrences of one code in one
// file is a different state from one, and a set would silently admit the second.
type RustcDebtRow {
  key: RustcDebtKey,
  multiplicity: Int
}

// WHICH OF THE THREE POPULATIONS A MALFORMEDNESS IS ABOUT. It is a closed coproduct rather than
// a String because the three have DIFFERENT owners and different repairs: the observation is
// produced by the compile and a defect there is an instrument defect; the ledger is authored in
// this repository and is the author's to edit; the baseline is content-addressed prior state and
// a defect there means the wrong baseline was loaded. A String would have let a reader guess.
type RustcDebtPopulation = ObservedPopulation | LedgerPopulation | BaselinePopulation

fn rustc_debt_population_render(p: RustcDebtPopulation) -> String {
  match p {
    ObservedPopulation => "observed"
    LedgerPopulation => "ledger"
    BaselinePopulation => "baseline"
  }
}

// WHY THE VERDICT IS A COPRODUCT AND NOT A BOOL. A refusal must say WHICH relation failed and
// over WHICH identity, or the author reads "the ratchet refused" and has to re-derive the cause
// by hand. The arms are the failure modes, each carrying what locates it.
type RustcDebtVerdict
  = DebtAdmitted { occurrences: Int, buckets: Int }
  | ObservedDivergesFromLedger { unledgered: List<RustcDebtKey>, stale: List<RustcDebtKey>, multiplicity_mismatch: List<RustcDebtKey> }
  | LedgerExceedsBaseline { grown: List<RustcDebtKey> }
  | DebtPopulationMalformed { population: RustcDebtPopulation, cause: String, keys: List<RustcDebtKey> }

fn rustc_debt_key_equal(left: RustcDebtKey, right: RustcDebtKey) -> Bool {
  left.source_path == right.source_path && left.code == right.code
}

fn rustc_debt_key_render(k: RustcDebtKey) -> String {
  "{k.source_path}:{k.code}"
}

fn rustc_debt_row_for_key(rows: List<RustcDebtRow>, key: RustcDebtKey) -> RustcDebtRow? {
  fold(
    rows,
    init: none,
    f: fn(acc, row) {
      match acc {
        Present { value: found } => Present { value: found }
        Absent => if rustc_debt_key_equal(left: row.key, right: key) {
          Present { value: row }
        } else {
          none
        }
      }
    }
  )
}

fn rustc_debt_multiplicity(rows: List<RustcDebtRow>, key: RustcDebtKey) -> Int {
  match rustc_debt_row_for_key(rows: rows, key: key) {
    Present { value: row } => row.multiplicity
    Absent => 0
  }
}

// A ROW WITH A NONPOSITIVE MULTIPLICITY IS MALFORMED, NOT EMPTY. Zero would be a row asserting
// that a key is admitted zero times, which is what ABSENCE already says; admitting both
// spellings gives one fact two representations and lets a stale-allowance refusal be dodged by
// editing a count to zero instead of deleting the row (DESIGN section 3).
fn rustc_debt_nonpositive_keys(rows: List<RustcDebtRow>) -> List<RustcDebtKey> {
  fold(
    rows,
    init: [],
    f: fn(acc, row) {
      if row.multiplicity > 0 { acc } else { append(acc, items: [row.key]) }
    }
  )
}

fn rustc_debt_key_occurrences(rows: List<RustcDebtRow>, key: RustcDebtKey) -> Int {
  fold(rows, init: 0, f: fn(n, other) {
    if rustc_debt_key_equal(left: key, right: other.key) { n + 1 } else { n }
  })
}

fn rustc_debt_duplicate_keys(rows: List<RustcDebtRow>) -> List<RustcDebtKey> {
  fold(
    rows,
    init: [],
    f: fn(acc, row) {
      if rustc_debt_key_occurrences(rows: rows, key: row.key) > 1 {
        append(acc, items: [row.key])
      } else {
        acc
      }
    }
  )
}

// CANONICAL ORDER IS STRICTLY ASCENDING BY RENDERED KEY, AND IT REFUSES RATHER THAN SORTS.
// Sorting here would be the absorbing widen: an out-of-order population is a producer that does
// not canonicalize, and quietly repairing it destroys the only signal that says so, while making
// two textually different ledgers compare equal so a diff stops being reviewable.
//
// The scan carries the PREVIOUS rendered key rather than indexing, so it is one linear pass and
// the first row -- which has no predecessor -- is distinguished by the sentinel being empty
// rather than by a position test. Every rendered key is nonempty (it contains a colon), so no
// real key can collide with the sentinel.
type RustcDebtOrderScan {
  previous: String,
  disordered: List<RustcDebtKey>
}

fn rustc_debt_disordered_keys(rows: List<RustcDebtRow>) -> List<RustcDebtKey> {
  fold(
    rows,
    init: { previous: "", disordered: [] },
    f: fn(acc, row) {
      let rendered = rustc_debt_key_render(k: row.key)
      if acc.previous != "" && !(acc.previous < rendered) {
        { previous: rendered, disordered: append(acc.disordered, items: [row.key]) }
      } else {
        { previous: rendered, disordered: acc.disordered }
      }
    }
  ).disordered
}

// ONE POPULATION'S OWN WELLFORMEDNESS, ASKED OF ALL THREE. An earlier revision asked it of the
// LEDGER ONLY, which is a fail-open in the exact place a ratchet must not have one: a duplicated
// key in the OBSERVATION makes `rustc_debt_multiplicity` answer with whichever row the fold
// reached first, so the observation-versus-ledger comparison silently compares the wrong
// quantity and the run reads as held. Positivity and uniqueness are preconditions of the
// COMPARISON, not properties of one side of it.
fn rustc_debt_population_defect(
  population: RustcDebtPopulation,
  rows: List<RustcDebtRow>
) -> RustcDebtVerdict? {
  let nonpositive = rustc_debt_nonpositive_keys(rows: rows)
  let duplicates = rustc_debt_duplicate_keys(rows: rows)
  let disordered = rustc_debt_disordered_keys(rows: rows)
  if count(nonpositive) > 0 {
    Present { value: DebtPopulationMalformed {
      population: population,
      cause: "a row carries a nonpositive multiplicity; absence is how zero is spelled",
      keys: nonpositive
    } }
  } else {
    if count(duplicates) > 0 {
      Present { value: DebtPopulationMalformed {
        population: population,
        cause: "one (source_path, code) key appears more than once, so its multiplicity has two answers",
        keys: duplicates
      } }
    } else {
      if count(disordered) > 0 {
        Present { value: DebtPopulationMalformed {
          population: population,
          cause: "rows are not in strictly ascending rendered-key order, so the population is not canonical",
          keys: disordered
        } }
      } else {
        none
      }
    }
  }
}

// OBSERVED KEYS THE LEDGER DOES NOT ADMIT AT THE OBSERVED MULTIPLICITY.
fn rustc_debt_unledgered(observed: List<RustcDebtRow>, admitted: List<RustcDebtRow>) -> List<RustcDebtKey> {
  fold(
    observed,
    init: [],
    f: fn(acc, row) {
      if rustc_debt_multiplicity(rows: admitted, key: row.key) == 0 {
        append(acc, items: [row.key])
      } else {
        acc
      }
    }
  )
}

// LEDGER KEYS NOTHING OBSERVED ANY MORE: the repaired-but-allowance-retained state. It refuses
// rather than passing quietly, because a stale allowance is a slot a future regression can
// occupy with no ledger edit -- the ratchet's own teeth, filed down in advance.
fn rustc_debt_stale(observed: List<RustcDebtRow>, admitted: List<RustcDebtRow>) -> List<RustcDebtKey> {
  fold(
    admitted,
    init: [],
    f: fn(acc, row) {
      if rustc_debt_multiplicity(rows: observed, key: row.key) == 0 {
        append(acc, items: [row.key])
      } else {
        acc
      }
    }
  )
}

fn rustc_debt_multiplicity_mismatch(observed: List<RustcDebtRow>, admitted: List<RustcDebtRow>) -> List<RustcDebtKey> {
  fold(
    observed,
    init: [],
    f: fn(acc, row) {
      let admitted_count = rustc_debt_multiplicity(rows: admitted, key: row.key)
      if admitted_count > 0 && admitted_count != row.multiplicity {
        append(acc, items: [row.key])
      } else {
        acc
      }
    }
  )
}

// EVERY KEY WHOSE ADMITTED MULTIPLICITY EXCEEDS THE BASELINE'S. A key absent from the baseline
// has baseline multiplicity zero, so any admission of it grows -- which is the correct reading:
// a brand-new admitted identity IS a ledger increase against the baseline, and refusing it is
// the whole point of the second relation.
fn rustc_debt_grown(admitted: List<RustcDebtRow>, baseline: List<RustcDebtRow>) -> List<RustcDebtKey> {
  fold(
    admitted,
    init: [],
    f: fn(acc, row) {
      if row.multiplicity > rustc_debt_multiplicity(rows: baseline, key: row.key) {
        append(acc, items: [row.key])
      } else {
        acc
      }
    }
  )
}

fn rustc_debt_occurrences(rows: List<RustcDebtRow>) -> Int {
  fold(rows, init: 0, f: fn(acc, row) { acc + row.multiplicity })
}

// THE VERDICT. The order of the arms is the order of the questions: a malformed ledger cannot
// be compared at all, an observation that diverges from the ledger is the author's own head,
// and only then is the ledger measured against the baseline.
fn rustc_debt_verdict(
  observed: List<RustcDebtRow>,
  admitted: List<RustcDebtRow>,
  baseline: List<RustcDebtRow>
) -> RustcDebtVerdict {
  match rustc_debt_population_defect(population: ObservedPopulation, rows: observed) {
    Present { value: defect } => defect
    Absent => match rustc_debt_population_defect(population: LedgerPopulation, rows: admitted) {
      Present { value: defect } => defect
      Absent => match rustc_debt_population_defect(population: BaselinePopulation, rows: baseline) {
        Present { value: defect } => defect
        Absent => {
      let unledgered = rustc_debt_unledgered(observed: observed, admitted: admitted)
      let stale = rustc_debt_stale(observed: observed, admitted: admitted)
      let mismatch = rustc_debt_multiplicity_mismatch(observed: observed, admitted: admitted)
      if count(unledgered) > 0 || count(stale) > 0 || count(mismatch) > 0 {
        ObservedDivergesFromLedger {
          unledgered: unledgered,
          stale: stale,
          multiplicity_mismatch: mismatch
        }
      } else {
        let grown = rustc_debt_grown(admitted: admitted, baseline: baseline)
        if count(grown) > 0 {
          LedgerExceedsBaseline { grown: grown }
        } else {
          DebtAdmitted {
            occurrences: rustc_debt_occurrences(rows: observed),
            buckets: count(observed)
          }
        }
      }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE VERDICT IS READ AS ITSELF, AND THERE IS NO BOOLEAN COLLAPSE OF IT.
//
// An earlier revision carried `rustc_debt_verdict_admits(v) -> Bool`, matching every variant and
// mapping one to true and the rest to false. That is predicate dissolution: it takes a coproduct
// whose whole purpose is to say WHICH relation failed and over WHICH keys, and hands the consumer
// one bit -- so every consumer that refuses on it prints "the ratchet refused" and the author
// re-derives the cause by hand, which is the typed-cause-erased-at-the-consumer failure. Raised
// in review 55911 and correct.
//
// What replaces it is a RENDERING, not a decision: the variant's own name, for a consumer that
// needs to say which state it is in (a test asserting the discriminating refusal, a receipt).
// A consumer that must ACT on the verdict matches the coproduct directly and keeps the payload,
// because the payload is the actionable half.
fn rustc_debt_verdict_tag(v: RustcDebtVerdict) -> String {
  match v {
    DebtAdmitted { occurrences: _, buckets: _ } => "DebtAdmitted"
    ObservedDivergesFromLedger { unledgered: _, stale: _, multiplicity_mismatch: _ } => "ObservedDivergesFromLedger"
    LedgerExceedsBaseline { grown: _ } => "LedgerExceedsBaseline"
    DebtPopulationMalformed { population: _, cause: _, keys: _ } => "DebtPopulationMalformed"
  }
}

// THE RECEIPT LINE. Both totals appear here and NEITHER reaches the verdict above; they are for
// a reader watching the trajectory, and printing them beside a disposition the totals did not
// compute is deliberate -- a number that decides nothing cannot rot into an oracle.
fn rustc_debt_receipt_line(v: RustcDebtVerdict) -> String {
  match v {
    DebtAdmitted { occurrences: o, buckets: b } =>
      "v2-rustc-debt: occurrences={o} buckets={b} disposition=held"
    ObservedDivergesFromLedger { unledgered: u, stale: s, multiplicity_mismatch: m } =>
      "v2-rustc-debt: REFUSED observation diverges from ledger unledgered={count(u)} stale={count(s)} multiplicity_mismatch={count(m)}"
    LedgerExceedsBaseline { grown: g } =>
      "v2-rustc-debt: REFUSED ledger exceeds baseline grown={count(g)}"
    DebtPopulationMalformed { population: p, cause: c, keys: k } =>
      "v2-rustc-debt: REFUSED {rustc_debt_population_render(p: p)} population malformed ({count(k)} key(s)): {c}"
  }
}
